    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d326">

                <pb facs="0566" n="434" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg434"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.515/7–1445: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 326<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-14T21:00:00+03:00">July 14, 1945—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2570. <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        Safehaven</hi>.</p>
                <p>
                    Emb has not rec’d any reply (ReDeptel 1475, June 30<anchor n="l"
                        xml:id="d326-f1"/>) to its <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        Safehaven
                    </hi> notes to FonOff of April 30 (London tel
                    4254, April 26<anchor n="2" xml:id="d326-f2"/>) and June 18 (ReEmbtel 2169, June 19<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d326-f3"/>) or any indication of desire to discuss matter.</p>
                <p>We believe Russians likely to persist in refusal to assent to general freezing of
                    state as well as private assets unless strong pressure can be brought to bear or
                    arguments adduced more forceful from Russian point of view than fact that
                    general freeze is technically more practicable (sent Dept as 2570 rptd London as
                    360).</p>
                <p>Russian aim is naturally to obtain for Soviet Govt maximum effective power of
                    disposition over all these assets. Russians wish private assets blocked and
                    placed at disposition of ACCs because they
                    control all ACCs and only in this way can they
                    effectively guard against disposal over these assets by private individuals
                    without their knowledge or consent. They do not wish state assets blocked
                    because they effectively control govts in question and are thus able to control
                    all dispositions over these assets. Hence they have a clear material interest in
                    arrangement they have proposed whereby private assets would be blocked and
                    released only on orders of respective ACCs and
                    state assets would not be blocked at all, and this interest will be uppermost in
                    their minds in any consideration of these questions.</p>
                <p>In view of above Emb would prefer to discuss
                    entire problem with Rubin who is expected
                    here shortly before recommending further approach to FonOff.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
